            <ArticleTitle>Reactions to Racial Trespassing.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>93-101</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1080/00224545.1978.9924149</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>Three trespassers into the &quot;body territory&quot; of racial groups-a white woman with an Afro wig, a black with a blond wig, and a Japanese American with a blond wig-invoked reactions among 144 white, black, and Japanese American female university students. The major findings were as follows: (a) Dominant group trespassing was more likely to be viewed in cultural perspectives, while minority trespassing was viewed in a racial context; (b) Minority members tended to view minority trespassing more negatively than dominant group trespassing.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
